{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"celadon-computable-smelts","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Symptom\n\nThere is no path by which a contributor can add a justified new mechanism. The\n`# mechanism-ok: <kind> <name> — <reason>` marker admits the mechanism at the\n`check_mechanism_delta.py --check` level (so `make lint` passes), but two tests in\n`tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py` assert conditions the marker does not affect:\n\n- `test_check_passes_on_the_committed_tree` asserts `new == 0`\n- `test_the_committed_baseline_matches_the_live_tree` asserts `set(baseline) == live`\n\nA marked-but-unbaselined mechanism satisfies `--check` and fails both tests. Measured on a real\nchange adding one justified `ci_gate`:\n\n```\nFAILED tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py::test_check_passes_on_the_committed_tree\n  - AssertionError: active=682 new=1 increased=0 stale=0\nFAILED tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py::test_the_committed_baseline_matches_the_live_tree\n  - Extra items in the right set: 'ci_gate::scripts/check_bare_repository_discovery.py'\n2 failed, 29 passed\n```\n\n## Why there is no sanctioned exit\n\nThe only way to make `set(baseline) == live` true is to add the entry to\n`.github/mechanism-baseline.json`. Every documented route to that is closed to a contributor:\n\n- **Hand-editing the baseline** — AGENTS.md: \"Do **not** hand-edit `.github/mechanism-baseline.json`\".\n- **`--update-stale`** — AGENTS.md: \"maintenance-only, and it refuses to write at all while\n  anything is new\". Since the entry IS new, it refuses by construction.\n- **`--lock`** — AGENTS.md: \"`--lock`/`--update-stale` are maintenance-only, not a contributor's\n  escape hatch.\"\n\nSo the marker's documented purpose (\"The gate does not forbid a new mechanism; it forbids an\n*unjustified* one\") is not achievable through any contributor-available action. In practice the\ngate forbids **all** new mechanisms, justified or not, and the marker only changes which of the\nthree checks fails.\n\n## Evidence this is costing real work\n\nTwo agents hit this independently on the same day, on unrelated bugs:\n\n1. Bug `9a17-e0b3-7aa6-4091` (e2e toolchain provisioning) added a dedicated workflow step, which\n   the detector counted as a new `ci_gate`. It added a marker, saw `--check` pass, then found these\n   two tests failing anyway. It **removed the marker and restructured**: the cache step became a\n   `uses:` (not a `ci_gate` at all) and provisioning was folded into an existing step whose name\n   was unchanged. Landed with no marker in the tree.\n2. Bug `740d-187c-53a2-4b7d` (bare-repository discovery sweep) added\n   `scripts/check_bare_repository_discovery.py` wired into `make lint` — a shape its plan reviewer\n   explicitly steered it toward, since the repo's established sweep idiom is\n   `scripts/check_*.py` + `make lint`. It added a justified marker, verified the marker was\n   load-bearing (`admitted` with it, `a mechanism was added with no justification` without it),\n   saw `make lint` exit 0, and pushed. The change fails CI on the two tests above.\n\nThe second case is the important one: the design is **correct** and matches the repo's own prior\nart, and the ratchet still blocks it. The pressure is toward contorting a design to dodge a\ndetector rather than toward justifying a mechanism, which inverts the ratchet's stated intent.\n\nNote also that `make lint` and the unit tests **disagree**, so a contributor following the\ndocumented verification (`make lint`) gets a false green and only discovers the problem from CI.\nThat gap is independently worth closing.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nDeterministic; verifiable in one run.\n\n1. There is a documented, contributor-available way to land a justified new mechanism. Adding one\n   with a valid `# mechanism-ok:` marker makes `check_mechanism_delta.py --check` pass **and**\n   `tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py` pass, with no hand-edit of the baseline and no\n   `--lock` / `--update-stale` / `--force`. Demonstrate end to end by adding a throwaway mechanism\n   with a marker, observing all three green, then removing it.\n2. An **unjustified** new mechanism (no marker, or a marker with a blank reason) still fails —\n   demonstrate it going RED. The ratchet must not be weakened into a no-op; that is the whole\n   point of the mechanism, and AC1 must not be satisfied by simply deleting the assertions.\n3. `make lint` and `tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py` agree on any given tree. A contributor\n   running the documented verification must not get a green that CI then contradicts.\n4. AGENTS.md's mechanism-delta section states the resulting path explicitly, including who may\n   perform any maintainer-only step, so the next contributor is not left inferring it.\n\nIf the intended design really is that a maintainer must run `--lock` to admit each new mechanism,\nthen that is the answer — but it must be written down in AGENTS.md as the path, and AC3 still\napplies so the contributor learns it from `make lint` rather than from a CI failure.\n\n## File impact\n\n`scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py` and/or `scripts/_mechanism_delta/`, `tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py`, `AGENTS.md`, possibly `docs/architecture.md` (the \"Mechanism-delta ratchet\" section).","id":"a1f1-a30d-2d50-4f5a","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["bug","ci","ratchet","dx","tooling"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Mechanism-delta ratchet has no contributor path to add a justified mechanism: the mechanism-ok marker satisfies --check but two unit tests still fail, and every route to the baseline is maintenance-only"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788489460529411001,"uuid":"919ff603-a323-40ac-b9ab-a97f915034fc"}